= Practice Questions (Exam Pattern)

Ten questions designed in authentic CUET UG single-correct style. The official marking scheme awards $+5$ for each correct answer and deducts $-1$ for every incorrect answer.

#mcq(1, [The nationwide 20-day All-India Railway Strike in May 1974 was organized and led by which prominent trade union leader?], (
  [A.K. Gopalan], [George Fernandes], [Morarji Desai], [Charan Singh],
))

#mcq(2, [In April 1973, which judge was appointed as the Chief Justice of India by superseding three senior Supreme Court judges?], (
  [Justice J.M. Shelat], [Justice A.N. Ray], [Justice H.R. Khanna], [Justice V.R. Krishna Iyer],
))

#mcq(3, [The Navnirman student movement of January 1974 against rising food prices and corruption occurred in which state?], (
  [Bihar], [Gujarat], [Uttar Pradesh], [Punjab],
))

#mcq(4, [Who gave the clarion call for "Total Revolution" (Sampoorna Kranti) during the Bihar student agitation of 1974?], (
  [Dr. Rammanohar Lohia], [Jayaprakash Narayan], [Acharya Narendra Deva], [K. Kamaraj],
))

#mcq(5, [On 12 June 1975, which High Court judge invalidated the 1971 election of Prime Minister Indira Gandhi on grounds of corrupt electoral practices?], (
  [Justice J.L. Sinha], [Justice A.N. Ray], [Justice P.N. Bhagwati], [Justice J.C. Shah],
))

#mcq(6, [The National Emergency on the night of 25 June 1975 was proclaimed by President Fakhruddin Ali Ahmed under which constitutional provision?], (
  [Article 356 on grounds of failure of constitutional machinery], [Article 360 on grounds of financial emergency], [Article 352 on grounds of "internal disturbance"], [Article 352 on grounds of "armed rebellion"],
))

#mcq(7, [Which newspaper chose to protest executive pre-censorship during the Emergency by leaving its editorial columns completely blank?], (
  [The Hindu], [The Indian Express], [The Times of India], [National Herald],
))

#mcq(8, [The controversial 42nd Constitutional Amendment Act of 1976 extended the term of the Lok Sabha and State Legislative Assemblies to:], (
  [5 years], [6 years], [7 years], [4 years],
))

#mcq(9, [In the March 1977 General Elections, which newly formed umbrella political party defeated the ruling Congress party to form India's first non-Congress central government?], (
  [Swatantra Party], [Bharatiya Jana Sangh], [Janata Party], [Congress for Democracy],
))

#mcq(10, [Which Constitutional Amendment Act replaced the term "internal disturbance" with "armed rebellion" and prohibited the suspension of Article 21 during an Emergency?], (
  [42nd Amendment Act (1976)], [44th Amendment Act (1978)], [52nd Amendment Act (1985)], [61st Amendment Act (1988)],
))

= Detailed Solutions

#solution(1, [George Fernandes led the National Coordination Committee for Railwaymen’s Struggle in May 1974, bringing the entire railway network to a standstill for 20 days.], answer: [B])

#solution(2, [Justice A.N. Ray was appointed Chief Justice of India in April 1973, superseding three senior judges (Shelat, Hegde, and Grover) who had ruled against the government in the Kesavananda Bharati case.], answer: [B])

#solution(3, [The Navnirman Movement took place in Gujarat in early 1974, triggered by hostel mess fee hikes, leading to Morarji Desai's fast and the dissolution of the state assembly.], answer: [B])

#solution(4, [Jayaprakash Narayan (JP) accepted the leadership of the Bihar student movement and issued the historic call for "Total Revolution" (Sampoorna Kranti) across all walks of society.], answer: [B])

#solution(5, [Justice Jagmohan Lal Sinha of the Allahabad High Court delivered the landmark verdict on 12 June 1975 in the election petition filed by Raj Narain.], answer: [A])

#solution(6, [The 1975 Emergency was proclaimed under Article 352 on the ground of "internal disturbance". "Armed rebellion" was only substituted later by the 44th Amendment in 1978.], answer: [C])

#solution(7, [The Indian Express and The Statesman famously registered silent defiance against state censorship by publishing blank editorial columns.], answer: [B])

#solution(8, [The 42nd Constitutional Amendment Act of 1976 extended the tenure of the Lok Sabha and Vidhan Sabhas from 5 years to 6 years (later restored to 5 years by the 44th Amendment).], answer: [B])

#solution(9, [The Janata Party was formed in early 1977 by the merger of Congress (O), Jana Sangh, Bharatiya Lok Dal, and Socialists, winning 295 seats and sweeping to power under Morarji Desai.], answer: [C])

#solution(10, [The 44th Constitutional Amendment Act of 1978 enacted by the Janata government introduced vital safeguards, including substituting "armed rebellion" for "internal disturbance" and making Article 20 and 21 inviolable.], answer: [B])

